Local patches
-------------

There are exactly **two** local modifications, kept in patches/ so they can be
replayed, in numeric order, after every upstream update. LICENSE is unmodified.

  patches/0001-allow-ctrl-c-interception-to-be-disabled.patch

    What: adds App::set_intercept_ctrl_c(bool) / App::intercepts_ctrl_c() and
    guards the "Mandatory Global Exit (Ctrl+C)" branch of App::run() with the
    new flag. The flag defaults to true, so upstream behaviour is unchanged for
    any code that does not call the setter.

    Why: upstream App::run() catches Ctrl+C at the top of the dispatch loop and
    quits unconditionally — before register_key callbacks, before the focused
    widget, before the exit keys. Only a live text selection makes it fall
    through (Ctrl+C then means Copy). calaos_config edits the Calaos
    configuration and must ask "discard unsaved changes?" before quitting, so
    it needs Ctrl+C to reach its own handler. With the flag cleared, Ctrl+C
    continues down the normal dispatch path; Copy-on-selection is unaffected in
    both modes.

    Upstream status: proposed, not yet merged. patches/UPSTREAM.md holds the
    ready-to-send issue/PR text. Drop the patch as soon as an equivalent knob
    is released upstream.

  patches/0002-copy-registered-key-before-invoking-callback.patch

    What: in step "1.7 Registered Key Events" of App::run(), copies the
    RegisteredKey out of key_events_ before calling it, and reads `consume`
    from that copy instead of from the map node. Three added lines plus a
    comment; dispatch order and consume semantics are unchanged.

    Why: upstream calls the callback through the unordered_map iterator
    (it->second.callback()) and then re-reads it->second.consume. A callback
    that calls unregister_key() on its own key erases that node, so the
    std::function is destroyed while its operator() is still on the stack and
    the consume read afterwards touches freed memory; register_key() from a
    callback is the same hazard via a rehash. Our own TUI hits it: the '?',
    'i', 'v', 'q' and 's' callbacks of calaos_config go through openDialog()
    -> updateShortcutState(), which unregisters those very letters. Both
    faults are reproducible under -fsanitize=address against the pristine
    header and silent with this patch.

    Upstream status: proposed, not yet merged. patches/UPSTREAM.md holds the
    ready-to-send issue text. Drop the patch as soon as the fix is released
    upstream.

Delete patches/UPSTREAM.md only once *both* patches are gone; until then, drop
just the section of it that belongs to the patch that was accepted.

The build uses the patched cpptui.hpp directly; there is no build-time patching
step. Do not "fix" the header any further without adding a numbered patch here:
an unrecorded local edit is silently lost at the next update.

Updating
--------

Run from this directory. It re-fetches upstream and re-applies every local
patch, in numeric order (0001 then 0002 — later patches are written against
the tree as the earlier ones left it), and stops loudly at the first one that
no longer applies — which means that patch must be rebased, or dropped because
upstream fixed it. Never let the loop continue past a failure: a half-patched
header compiles.

    set -e
    export LC_ALL=C   # so the patch glob sorts numerically
    SHA=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/jonoton/cpp-tui/commits/main \
          | sed -n 's/.*"sha": "\([0-9a-f]\{40\}\)".*/\1/p' | head -1)
    curl -fL -o cpptui.hpp https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jonoton/cpp-tui/$SHA/cpptui.hpp
    curl -fL -o LICENSE    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jonoton/cpp-tui/$SHA/LICENSE

    # record the pristine sha256 before patching
    sha256sum cpptui.hpp

    for p in patches/[0-9]*.patch; do
      echo "applying $p"
      patch -p1 -d ../../.. --batch --forward --no-backup-if-mismatch < "$p" || {
        echo "FAILED to apply $p — rebase it or drop it, do not skip it" >&2
        exit 1
      }
    done

    sha256sum cpptui.hpp   # patched value

Then update the commit, date, sizes and both sha256 above, and re-check the
"Local patches" section (a patch that upstream has merged must be deleted, not
kept as a no-op).

Usage notes
-----------

Header-only, no dependency, no object file: only an include path is needed
(-I$(top_srcdir)/src/lib/cpptui, added to src/bin/tools/Makefile.am).

cpptui.hpp must only be included from .cpp files of the TUI, never from a
header exposed to the rest of the tree: it is 18k lines and it costs roughly
+10 s of compile time and +650 MB of compiler RSS per translation unit that
includes it (measured with the project's own CXXFLAGS, gcc 12, aarch64).

The header produces 30 warnings with the project's warning flags (29 -Wshadow,
1 -Wunused-variable). Include it through a thin local wrapper that wraps the
#include in `#pragma GCC diagnostic push / ignored "-Wshadow" / ignored
"-Wunused-variable" / pop`, so the tree stays at zero warnings without
weakening the flags for our own code.

On Unix, the input parser remaps control characters 1..26 to `c + 96`, so
Ctrl+C is delivered as `key == 'c'` with `ctrl == true`, not as `key == 3`.
Code opting out of the Ctrl+C interception must therefore register
`register_key('c', cb, /*ctrl=*/true)`; `register_key(3, ...)` never fires
here, even though `Event::is_copy()` also accepts key 3.
